{"id":1964,"date":"2026-09-07T12:15:52","date_gmt":"2026-09-07T04:15:52","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/?p=1964"},"modified":"2026-09-07T12:17:38","modified_gmt":"2026-09-07T04:17:38","slug":"why-rotary-hearth-furnaces-need-a-different-kind-of-lining","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/why-rotary-hearth-furnaces-need-a-different-kind-of-lining\/","title":{"rendered":"Why Rotary Hearth Furnaces Need a Different Kind of Lining"},"content":{"rendered":"\t\t<div data-elementor-type=\"wp-post\" data-elementor-id=\"1964\" class=\"elementor elementor-1964\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-f2661d3 e-con-full e-flex e-con e-parent\" data-id=\"f2661d3\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-settings=\"{&quot;content_width&quot;:&quot;full&quot;}\" data-core-v316-plus=\"true\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-72c610f elementor-widget__width-initial elementor-widget elementor-widget-image\" data-id=\"72c610f\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"image.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t<style>\/*! elementor - v3.19.0 - 28-02-2024 *\/\n.elementor-widget-image{text-align:center}.elementor-widget-image a{display:inline-block}.elementor-widget-image a img[src$=\".svg\"]{width:48px}.elementor-widget-image img{vertical-align:middle;display:inline-block}<\/style>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1080\" height=\"1080\" src=\"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth.png\" class=\"attachment-full size-full wp-image-1966\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth.png 1080w, https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth-1024x1024.png 1024w, https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/Hearth-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1080px) 100vw, 1080px\" \/>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-75f36bf elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor\" data-id=\"75f36bf\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"text-editor.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t<style>\/*! elementor - v3.19.0 - 28-02-2024 *\/\n.elementor-widget-text-editor.elementor-drop-cap-view-stacked .elementor-drop-cap{background-color:#69727d;color:#fff}.elementor-widget-text-editor.elementor-drop-cap-view-framed .elementor-drop-cap{color:#69727d;border:3px solid;background-color:transparent}.elementor-widget-text-editor:not(.elementor-drop-cap-view-default) .elementor-drop-cap{margin-top:8px}.elementor-widget-text-editor:not(.elementor-drop-cap-view-default) .elementor-drop-cap-letter{width:1em;height:1em}.elementor-widget-text-editor .elementor-drop-cap{float:left;text-align:center;line-height:1;font-size:50px}.elementor-widget-text-editor .elementor-drop-cap-letter{display:inline-block}<\/style>\t\t\t\t<header aria-label=\"Article header\"><\/header><div><p>A static furnace and a rotary hearth furnace can run at the same temperature. But they are not the same problem for the lining.<\/p><p>In a static furnace, the lining deals with heat: thermal expansion, contraction, chemical attack from the atmosphere or the product. These are well-understood stresses and there are reliable materials for handling them. In a rotary hearth, the lining moves. Every rotation puts tensile forces through the blanket that a static installation never sees. That changes which materials are actually suited to the job.<\/p><p>We recently lined a rotary hearth furnace used for ceramic granulate annealing, operating at temperatures up to 1500\u00b0C on a continuous basis. The customer had two problems with the existing lining, and both were predictable once you understood what was happening.<\/p><p>The first was contamination. The previous material contained organic binders. At first heat-up, those binders burned off and the gases entered the process atmosphere. For ceramic granulate annealing, where product quality depends on a clean, controlled atmosphere, that contamination caused scrap. It happened every time the furnace came back from a cold shutdown.<\/p><p>The second problem was shrinkage. The previous lining shrank enough at operating temperature that gaps opened between the sections. On a static furnace, a small gap is manageable. On a rotating hearth, the gap opens and closes with every revolution. That drives temperature variation across the hearth and, eventually, structural failure at the joints as the edges take the load.<\/p><p>VSG NF-1600 addressed both directly. Sol-gel manufacturing produces short alumina-silica fibers with no organic components, so there are no binders to burn off. The process atmosphere stays clean from the first heat-up.<\/p><p>On shrinkage, NF-1600 stays below 1% linear shrinkage at 1500\u00b0C and below 2% at 1600\u00b0C. On a rotating hearth those numbers matter because the joints need to stay tight under continuous movement. A lining that shrinks significantly at operating temperature will open gaps regardless of how well it was installed.<\/p><p>Double-sided needling gives the blanket tensile strength in both directions through the thickness, so it absorbs the pull forces from rotation rather than tearing or delaminating at the surface.<\/p><p>For this application we selected the 128 kg\/m\u00b3 density, which gives a thermal conductivity of 0.099 W\/(m\u00b7K) at 800\u00b0C. That balances insulation performance with the structural integrity the rotating application requires.<\/p><p>If you are specifying a lining for a rotary hearth, the failure modes are specific and the material selection needs to reflect them. We are happy to share the full technical data for NF-1600 and to work through density and thickness selection for your operating conditions.<\/p><\/div>\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>A static furnace and a rotary hearth furnace can run at the same temperature. But they are not the same problem for the lining. In a static furnace, the lining deals with heat: thermal expansion, contraction, chemical attack from the atmosphere or the product. These are well-understood stresses and there are reliable materials for handling [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"om_disable_all_campaigns":false,"_monsterinsights_skip_tracking":false,"cybocfi_hide_featured_image":"","footnotes":"","_links_to":"","_links_to_target":""},"categories":[1],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1964","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-news"],"acf":[],"aioseo_notices":[],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/1964","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/2"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=1964"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/1964\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1973,"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/1964\/revisions\/1973"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1964"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.vulcanshield.